# 180Connect environment variables
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the values:
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored and must never be committed. This file is the
# documented list of what the app needs — keep it in sync with the schema in
# `src/lib/env.ts`. See `docs/environment-variables.md` for details.
#
# Ask the project admin for the real values.


# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The server refuses to start without these.

# Absolute base URL this deployment is served from.
# Local development: http://localhost:3000
# Production: the real domain, no trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase project URL for this environment.
# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API -> Project URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# Supabase publishable key. Safe in the browser; access is still constrained by
# row-level security. Read by `src/lib/supabase/server.ts`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_your-key

# Cloudflare Turnstile site key for the login CAPTCHA (F003). Public by design:
# it only identifies the widget, and validating a token needs the paired secret
# that lives in Supabase (Authentication -> Attack Protection). The value below
# is Cloudflare's official always-pass test key — correct for local development
# and CI. Staging and production use the real key from the Cloudflare dashboard.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA


# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Consumed today, but each has a working default or an alternative.

# Environment label. Set to `staging` to show a banner on every page warning
# that data may change or reset and the app can be briefly unstable during
# deploys — for external accounts on a staging/preview link. Leave unset (or
# `production`/`local`) to show no banner.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ENV=

# Legacy name for the publishable key (F223). Read as a fallback by
# `src/lib/supabase/server.ts`; prefer NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
# above. Set one or the other — the server will not start with neither.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Email domain(s) users may sign in from — one, or a comma-separated list.
# Server-only — do not prefix this value with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Defaults to 180dc.org.
#
# This is only half the rule, and the friendlier half. Postgres is what actually
# decides: app.allowed_email_domains, read by a trigger on auth.users
# (20260804110000). This variable exists so a wrong address gets a sentence from
# the form instead of a database error, and so an environment permitting an extra
# domain for testing does not have its own login form refuse it.
#
# Keep the two in step. Widening this alone can never let anybody in — it only
# changes the message shown. To actually permit a domain, add the row too; see
# docs/auth/invite-email.md.
#   AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAIN=180dc.org,example.com
AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAIN=180dc.org

# Password-recovery link lifetime in seconds. 24 hours (F010) — keep this
# aligned with the OTP expiry configured in Supabase Auth, which is shared with
# invite links; see docs/auth/invite-email.md.
PASSWORD_RESET_WINDOW_SECONDS=86400

# SECRET. Cloudflare Turnstile secret key, the server half of the pair above
# (F003). The deployed app never reads it — Supabase holds it and validates the
# token — but `supabase/config.toml` reads it when running the local Supabase
# stack. Use Cloudflare's documented always-pass test secret locally:
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing/
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=


# --- Not yet required -------------------------------------------------------
# Declared so the list is complete, but nothing consumes them yet. Each becomes
# required in `src/lib/env.ts` when the feature that uses it is merged.

# SECRET. Supabase service-role key (F223).
# Bypasses row-level security. Server-side only — never add a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix to this, and never read it from a client component.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# SECRET. Key used to sign the inactivity record behind session expiry (F007).
# Server-side only — never add a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. Leave unset locally and
# sessions still expire after 30 idle minutes; set it in staging and production
# so the record cannot be forged by a replayed session. Generate with
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Changing it signs every open session out once.
SESSION_ACTIVITY_SECRET=

# SECRET. Postgres connection string for the database `npm run seed` (and
# `npm run seed:clear`) writes to (F233). Only the seed scripts read this — the
# app never does.
# Use the SESSION POOLER string: dashboard -> Connect -> Session pooler. It is
# IPv4 (works everywhere) and behaves like a direct connection. The direct
# (db.<ref>.supabase.co) string is IPv6-only and will fail on most machines.
#   Session pooler: postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<pw>@aws-1-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#   Local stack:    postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres
# The scripts refuse to run if this points at the production project.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# SECRET. Shared secret checked by the scheduled-send route handler before it
# does any work, so the endpoint cannot be triggered by anyone who finds the
# URL. See Q-02 in docs/open-questions.md.
CRON_SECRET=

# Sentry DSN — where captured application errors are sent (F226).
# Public by design (a DSN only permits writing events), so it carries the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and is safe in the browser bundle. Sentry dashboard ->
# Project Settings -> Client Keys (DSN). Leave unset to log errors to the
# platform console instead of Sentry.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# Environment tag applied to captured errors: "staging" or "production" (F226).
# Optional — falls back to Vercel's VERCEL_ENV when unset, so staging (preview)
# and production are distinguished automatically.
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=
# SECRET. Resend API key for transactional platform email — the F008 invite today,
# notification mail later. Server-side only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# Leave it unset and nothing is sent: `src/lib/email/send.ts` logs each message
# to the console instead, so local development and CI cannot email a real person.
# Client outreach does NOT go through this — PRD §12.1 sends that from each
# CAM's own authorised Gmail account.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Sender for transactional email, bare address or `Name <address>`.
# The domain must be verified in the Resend dashboard. Resend's own
# onboarding@resend.dev is not a substitute: it only ever delivers to the
# account owner's address, so an invite to anyone else is rejected with a 403.
# Required whenever RESEND_API_KEY is set.
EMAIL_FROM=180 Connect <no-reply@your-verified-domain.example>
# Comma-separated addresses or bare domains transactional email may reach.
# Anything else is dropped and logged instead of delivered. Unset means no
# restriction — that is the production setting. Set it wherever a real charity
# contact could be emailed by mistake: staging, or a developer sending from a
# domain that is not 180DC's.
#   EMAIL_RECIPIENT_ALLOWLIST=180dc.org,someone@gmail.com
EMAIL_RECIPIENT_ALLOWLIST=
# SECRET. Companies House API key for company data ingestion (F038).
# HTTP Basic Auth — sent as the username with a blank password. Server-side only:
# only the ingestion runner reads it, and it must never carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# Get a "Live" REST key at https://developer.company-information.service.gov.uk
# (not "Test" — Companies House's own forum flags Test-environment auth as
# unreliable against the live search endpoint). Becomes required with F032.
COMPANIES_HOUSE_API_KEY=

# SECRET. CharityBase API key for charity data ingestion (F038/F031).
# GraphQL API — sent as `Authorization: Apikey <key>`. Server-side only, same rule
# as above. Get a key at https://charitybase.uk. Becomes required with F031, whose
# adapter is not merged yet.
CHARITYBASE_API_KEY=

# SECRET. Charity Commission API key for charity data ingestion (F038/F033).
# Sent as `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key`. Server-side only, same rule as above.
# Get a key at https://api-portal.charitycommission.gov.uk (free developer
# account required). Becomes required with F033, whose adapter is not merged
# yet.
CHARITY_COMMISSION_API_KEY=

# Optional. Start/end date (YYYY-MM-DD) bounds for the Charity Commission
# backfill (F033). Defaults to 2000-01-01 through today if unset — useful for
# testing against a narrow date range locally instead of the full register.
CHARITY_COMMISSION_BACKFILL_START=
CHARITY_COMMISSION_BACKFILL_END=

# No entry for 360Giving (F035): confirmed live 2026-08-10 that
# api.threesixtygiving.org needs no API key or auth header at all — signing
# up at 360giving.org/api-docs only subscribes you to email updates about the
# API, it does not issue a credential. Nothing to add here.

# SECRET. Google Gemini API key for LLM calls (F082 Client Booklet generation,
# and later F100 email drafts). Server-side only, same rule as above. Get a
# free-tier key at https://aistudio.google.com — per team decision, stay on
# the free tier until the product ships, then move to a paid key. Passed
# explicitly to the AI SDK rather than read from its default env var name, so
# every var this app reads stays declared in src/lib/env.ts. Becomes required
# with F082.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Gemini model id for booklet generation (F082) — copy the exact id shown in
# the Google AI Studio model picker for the key above (a Flash-tier model is
# the free-tier fit for this task; see "180Connect LLM Provider Research.docx.md").
# No hardcoded default in code: Google retires model ids often enough that
# guessing one here would go stale. Booklet generation cannot run until this
# is set.
GEMINI_MODEL=

# Gmail API outreach (F241/F124). Create a Web application OAuth client in the
# organisation-owned Google Cloud project. Authorise it while signed in as the
# dedicated outreach mailbox. These four values are an all-or-nothing set: the
# app refuses to use a fallback sender when configuration is incomplete.
GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=
GMAIL_SENDER_EMAIL=clients.sheffield@180dc.org

# F227 per-user cost and abuse control shared by every Gemini generation feature.
# Defaults to 20 requests per 3600-second fixed window. Server-only: changing
# either value adjusts the threshold without a code change.
AI_GENERATION_RATE_LIMIT=20
AI_GENERATION_RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600

# F227 per-user fixed window on outreach email sending (manual and scheduled).
# Defaults to 100 emails per 3600-second window. Server-only: changing either
# value adjusts the threshold without a code change.
EMAIL_SEND_RATE_LIMIT=100
EMAIL_SEND_RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600

# --- Local tooling only -----------------------------------------------------
# The application never reads these. They exist so the command-line procedures
# in docs/ can be followed without hunting through dashboards. Nothing here
# belongs in `src/lib/env.ts`.

# Host of the production session-mode pooler, used when running a backup or a
# restore test by hand (F225). The nightly workflow does not read this file —
# it reads a GitHub Actions *repository variable* of the same name, so if you
# change one, change the other. See docs/Backups/backup-setup.md step 2.
#
# Session mode (port 5432) only: transaction mode does not support pg_dump.
# The direct host (db.<ref>.supabase.co) is IPv6-only and will not work from
# CI at all — see section 0 of docs/Backups/F225-database-backups.md.
# Supabase dashboard -> Connect -> Session pooler; hostname only.
SUPABASE_PROD_POOLER_HOST=aws-0-eu-west-1.pooler.supabase.com
